# WHAT A STUDENT DOWNLOADS, and the only thing that decides it (issue #115).
#
# This repo IS the ~/cs193v layout. install-cs193v.sh downloads
# https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/archive/refs/heads/<branch>.tar.gz and unpacks it into the
# student's course directory. GitHub builds those archives with `git archive`, which honours
# export-ignore -- so every rule below is present for anyone working in the repo and absent from
# every student download. There is no other filter: no release step, no packaging script, no CI.
#
# .private/tests/11-export.sh asserts the result on every run of the suite, so this file and the
# file set it produces cannot drift apart. 00-release-gates.sh checks the same thing against the
# real GitHub endpoint, which is the one claim nothing local can prove.

# ─── the root: three files a student has no use for ────────────────────────────
#
# The course directory is deliberately kept to TWO visible items, the launcher and projects/
# (issue #16), so a developer-facing file sitting next to them would undo that.
#
# THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING on CLAUDE.md. Patterns here match like .gitignore ones:
# without it, `CLAUDE.md` matches that basename at ANY depth. There is no second CLAUDE.md in
# the tree today -- the agent notes live at .private/files/agent-notes.md and the Containerfile
# symlinks /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md at them during the build -- but an unanchored rule would
# silently strip one the day somebody adds it back under files/, and the build installs whatever
# is there. Anchored, only the root file goes.
#
# .gitattributes and .gitignore are inert in ~/cs193v, which is not a git repository: this file
# is distribution mechanics, and .gitignore exists to keep the launcher's per-machine tunnel keys
# out of commits. Neither has a reader on a student's machine.
/CLAUDE.md      export-ignore
/.gitattributes export-ignore
/.gitignore     export-ignore

# ─── .private/: an ALLOWLIST, not a list of exclusions ─────────────────────────
#
# Deny the directory and name the seven things that ship. This is the direction that fails safe:
# the next staff document, test fixture or scratch note is excluded automatically, where a
# list-the-exclusions rule would ship it until somebody remembered. It is the same argument
# lib/assert.sh makes for excluding .config wholesale -- "a name-them-all rule leaks the sixth
# file silently the day it appears."
#
# What ships, and why each one has to:
#
#   Containerfile   IS the distribution. There is no registry and no published image; the
#                   installer runs `./cs193v --rebuild` and the image is assembled on the
#                   student's own machine. See README.md, "How the image reaches a student".
#   messages.txt    every student-facing string the launcher prints.
#   course-install-messages.txt  every student-facing string the INSTALLER prints (#221).
#                   A sibling of messages.txt, not a copy of it: different keys, different file,
#                   and 10-static.sh asserts the two do not overlap.
#   course-install.sh  the installer proper (#221). install-cs193v.sh is the bootstrap a
#                   student downloads; it fetches this tree and execs this file out of it, so
#                   this one has to travel in the archive while the bootstrap must not. That
#                   is why export:no-installers greps for the BOOTSTRAP's name and not for
#                   "installer".
#   install-utils.sh  the installer's own shared half (#217): the package table and the steps
#                   that need root. It ships for the same reason course-install.sh does -- both
#                   of the scripts the bootstrap can exec source it out of the archive -- and it
#                   is NOT under files/ on purpose, because everything there is hashed into
#                   cs193v.buildhash and a package name would then prompt every student to
#                   rebuild a container that has no reader for it.
#   wsl-provision.sh  the root pass (#217). The Windows installer runs the bootstrap twice inside
#                   the CS193V WSL instance it creates: once as root, which execs this and
#                   prepares the instance, and once as the student, which execs
#                   course-install.sh. Nothing on a Mac or a Linux machine ever reads it.
#   files/          the whole of the rest of the build context. `COPY files/` is the only COPY
#                   in the Containerfile, and files/cs193v-ui.sh is a HARD requirement of every
#                   single ./cs193v invocation -- the launcher sources it before its dispatch
#                   case and exits 1 if it cannot be read.
#
# Containerfile, messages.txt and container.args are also the three files install_files checks
# for after untarring, alongside the launcher itself. (fetch_files, as it was called until #221,
# when the download moved into the bootstrap and this step became extraction only.)
#
# course-install-messages.txt IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THAT LIST. It ships because the archive is
# what the bootstrap unpacks and the installer reads its prose out of THAT copy -- but nothing in
# the student's finished tree ever opens it, so a tree missing it is still a working install and
# install_files must not refuse one. The bootstrap's own sentinel check is where it is required,
# because that is the copy that matters.
#
# BOTH files LINES ARE REQUIRED AND THIS IS THE EASIEST THING HERE TO BREAK. Measured, all three
# forms: with only `/.private/files/** -export-ignore` the archive holds NO files/ at all, and
# with only `/.private/files -export-ignore` it holds no files/ at all either. `/.private/**`
# matches the files DIRECTORY as well as its contents, and a pruned tree walk never reaches the
# children -- so the /** negation alone has nothing to act on, and the directory negation alone
# un-prunes a directory whose children are still ignored. Together they restore the subtree
# recursively, subdirectories included. Deleting either line ships a course directory whose
# ./cs193v cannot start.
#
#     git archive HEAD | tar -t | grep -c '^\.private/files/'     # expect 30
/.private/**              export-ignore
/.private/Containerfile  -export-ignore
/.private/messages.txt   -export-ignore
/.private/course-install.sh -export-ignore
/.private/course-install-messages.txt -export-ignore
/.private/install-utils.sh -export-ignore
/.private/wsl-provision.sh -export-ignore
/.private/files          -export-ignore
/.private/files/**       -export-ignore

# THE ICONS, AND NOTE WHAT IS **NOT** HERE: no `/.private/icons/** -export-ignore`.
#
# The files/ pair above needs its /** because everything under files/ ships. icons/ is the
# opposite case -- two of its four entries ship and two must not. src/ holds the 1024 and 824
# masters and make-icons.sh is what turns them into these two artifacts, and neither is any use
# on a student's machine: iconutil is macOS-only and install-cs193v-windows.cmd cannot build an
# .ico at all, which is why both outputs are committed rather than generated on demand.
#
# SO THE DIRECTORY IS UN-IGNORED AND THEN EACH FILE IS NAMED. The directory line is still
# required for the reason the files/ comment above measured -- `/.private/**` matches the icons
# DIRECTORY, and a pruned tree walk never reaches its children -- but the second half is per-file
# rather than a glob. Adding a `/**` here in the name of symmetry with files/ would ship the
# masters and the generator; 11-export.sh's export:no-icon-sources is what catches that.
#
#     git archive HEAD | tar -t | grep '^\.private/icons/'       # expect exactly these two
/.private/icons          -export-ignore
/.private/icons/cs193v.ico  -export-ignore

# cs193v.icns IS NO LONGER SHIPPED, and that is a change rather than an oversight. It used to be
# read at install time, when install_mac_app generated a bundle and copied the icon into it. The
# applet is now prebuilt and carries its own copy, so the only install-time reader left is the
# Windows shim, which wants the .ico. The .icns stays in the repo because make-macapp.sh reads
# it; it just stops travelling to students, which is 224 KB off the tree.

# ─── the prebuilt macOS applet  (#134) ────────────────────────────────────────
# THE BUNDLE SHIPS; WHAT BUILT IT DOES NOT. Same split as icons/: cs193v-app.applescript and
# make-macapp.sh are for reading and rebuilding on a staff Mac, and cs193v-run is copied INTO
# the bundle at build time so the loose copy would be a second, divergeable one. All three stay
# behind the `/.private/**` blanket; only the bundle is un-ignored.
#
# BOTH FORMS AGAIN, for the reason the files/ pair above records: `/.private/**` matches the
# macapp DIRECTORY, and a pruned walk never reaches its children.
# THREE LINES, NOT TWO, and the middle one is the one I first left out: `/.private/**` prunes
# the macapp directory AND the CS193V.app directory inside it, and a pruned walk never reaches
# the children -- so un-ignoring `CS193V.app/**` alone has nothing to act on. Measured: the
# archive contained `.private/macapp/` and not one file beneath it. The `/**` then covers every
# nested directory too, Contents/ and Contents/MacOS/ included, because ** crosses slashes.
/.private/macapp                 -export-ignore
/.private/macapp/CS193V.app      -export-ignore
/.private/macapp/CS193V.app/**   -export-ignore

# ─── the first binary assets in this repo  (#134) ──────────────────────────────
# EVERY OTHER TRACKED FILE HERE IS TEXT, which is why no rule covered these and why they need
# one. Without `binary`, git applies its text heuristics: on a machine with core.autocrlf set --
# the default on a Windows checkout, which is not hypothetical for a repo carrying a .cmd -- a
# byte pair inside compressed image data can be rewritten on checkout. That corrupts the file
# silently, and only on the machine that checked it out, so it cannot be reproduced anywhere else.
#
# `binary` IS A MACRO for `-text -diff -merge`, so it also stops git printing a megabyte of
# mojibake into a diff and stops it attempting a line merge on two different pictures.
*.png   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ico   binary

# THE APPLET NEEDS A PATH RULE, NOT AN EXTENSION ONE, and this is measured rather than assumed.
# Inside a signed bundle, Contents/Info.plist and Contents/_CodeSignature/CodeResources are XML
# TEXT with no useful extension, and under core.autocrlf git rewrites both -- after which
# `codesign -v` reports `invalid Info.plist`. The Mach-O itself survives, because git detects it
# as binary on its own, so the app still LAUNCHES in that state and nothing but a verify would
# notice. A bundle that fails to verify has a damaged identity, which is the one thing the applet
# exists to have. With this rule the round-trip is byte-identical and the seal stays VALID.
/.private/macapp/CS193V.app/** binary

# CRLF ON THE BATCH FILE IS LOAD-BEARING, and this is the only thing that keeps it.
#
# cmd.exe does not read a batch file line by line. It reads it in 512-BYTE CHUNKS, and its label
# scanner assumes a two-byte \r\n terminator. Under LF-only endings that scanner drifts, so
# `goto` and `call :label` fail depending on the BYTE OFFSET of the label -- inserting or deleting
# a byte anywhere earlier in the file makes the bug appear or vanish, and duplicating labels does
# not fix it. install-cs193v-windows.cmd is entirely `goto`-structured, so it is maximally
# exposed. It shipped LF-only until this rule existed.
#
# `text eol=crlf` and not `-text`: both produce CRLF today, but this one is self-healing. The repo
# stores LF, and CHECKOUT converts -- so a TA who commits from an editor that saved LF still
# publishes a working file, where `-text` would preserve the mistake verbatim.
#
# THE CHECKOUT IS THE ONLY PATH THAT MATTERS FOR THE TWO PUBLISHED FILES. install-cs193v.sh and
# install-cs193v-windows.cmd do not reach a student through the archive: both are hosted on the
# course website, and the .cmd fetches stage two from raw.githubusercontent.com, which serves the
# stored blob and honours no attribute. So the copy a TA uploads comes from a working tree. Do not
# verify this through `git archive`: the .cmd is export-ignored, so the archive holds nothing to
# look at.
#
# THE INSTALLER PROPER IS THE OTHER WAY ROUND, and since #221 that distinction is the whole
# shape of the thing. course-install.sh reaches a student ONLY through the archive -- the
# bootstrap downloads the tree and execs it out of there -- so it needs its line-ending
# attributes to be right in the ARCHIVE, where the two published files need theirs right in the
# checkout. Two files published by hand, one delivered by tarball, and they are verified in
# different places.
#
# Wine CANNOT catch a regression here: its cmd reads bare \n natively (batch.c:259-266), so the
# --tier windows run passes either way. 25-installer.sh asserts it statically instead, against
# the working-tree file, via cmdlint_line_endings.
#
#     file .private/install-cs193v-windows.cmd
#         # expect: ... with CRLF line terminators
*.cmd text eol=crlf
